{
  "_comment": [
    "The chip ABI as velxio-chip.h states it, one table for every host (board-buses F7).",
    "Three suites replay these scenarios against the same abi-probe.wasm, each driving the",
    "chip the way that host does in production: board-buses-abi-parity.test.ts (the browser,",
    "through the bus fabric and a PinManager), the OSS test_wasm_chip_abi_parity.py (the QEMU",
    "workers: spi_transfer_byte behind spi_cs_active, the I2C bus table and WasmChipI2CSlave,",
    "feed_uart_byte, notify_pin_change, fire_due_timers on a clock) and the pro",
    "test_board_buses_abi_parity_pi.py (the Linux boards, whole I2C transactions and the CE",
    "replay of PiSpiResponder). A step answers the same everywhere unless it carries `hosts`,",
    "which names the host that answers differently and says why: a host difference is stated,",
    "never skipped.",
    "",
    "Ops. call: a probe export, with `ret` when it returns something. drive: the MCU drives",
    "the pad a chip input is wired to (a digitalWrite: the pad reported as driving, and the",
    "level). pad: the MCU configures that pad as an input with a pull (a pinMode) and drives",
    "nothing. spi: the board's SPI controller clocks `mosi` and reads `miso`. i2c: the board's",
    "I2C controller runs `seq` (start addr read -> ack, write byte -> ack, read -> byte, stop).",
    "uart_rx: the board's UART transmits to the chip. board_pin: the level the board reads on",
    "the pin wired to a chip pin. clock: the guest clock reaches `ns` and every timer due by",
    "then fires. attr: the host applies a live attribute value. poke: bytes into the chip's",
    "scratch. trace: the callbacks the chip recorded since the last trace step, in order,",
    "then cleared.",
    "",
    "A scenario with `only` names the hosts that can drive it at all, and says why the others",
    "cannot: the Linux boards address a chip enable per transaction (a select is never floating",
    "volts: the tab's circuit solve publishes `volts` on the net of the pad wired to chip pin `pin` (null: no wire reaches the pad any more). The browser runtime reads it off the electrical store; a worker or Linux host is told it as pad_volts, keyed by the chip's pin names.",
    "and never a GPIO the guest writes) and forward no pins, UART or timers to a chip."
  ],
